The upstream repository is dual-licensed.  Its code is MIT.  Its content is CC BY-SA 4.0.  The frequency lists are content, so this file is CC BY-SA 4.0.


Attribution

Use this line wherever the file goes.

        Frequency list by Hermit Dave, from the FrequencyWords project, used
        under CC BY-SA 4.0.


Underlying corpus

The list is built from OpenSubtitles 2018.  That is a collection of film and television subtitles, gathered by the OPUS project from material contributed to opensubtitles.org.

        https://opus.nlpl.eu/OpenSubtitles2018.php

The usual academic citation is Pierre Lison and Jorg Tiedemann, "OpenSubtitles2016: Extracting Large Parallel Corpora from Movie and TV Subtitles", LREC 2016.


What share-alike means for this file

This repository hands out a copy of en_50k.txt.  That is redistribution.  Two rules follow.

        1. The attribution line above travels with the file.

        2. The file stays under CC BY-SA 4.0.  It cannot be relicensed.

Share-alike stops at the file.  It does not spread to the code.  bookbot.py and cefr_bands.py stay MIT.  A CC BY-SA file may sit beside MIT code.  Neither licence changes the other.


What you may publish

These carry no obligation.  None of them gives a reader any part of the word list.

        A report about your own manuscript.  Band percentages, coverage figures, grade levels, word counts.

        Your own exclusion list.  It comes out of your book, not out of this file.

        The band boundaries in cefr_bands.py.  DEFAULT_BANDS holds rank numbers, such as 1 to 1000 and 1001 to 2000.  Rank numbers are not list content.


What you may publish only under CC BY-SA 4.0

Each of these is the word list in another shape.  Publishing one publishes the list.

        A shortened list.  The top 5000 words, say, to save space.

        A filtered list, with some words dropped.

        A re-ranked or re-sorted list.

        This list merged with another word list.

        A lookup file built from the list, such as a saved word-to-rank table.

        A sample word list shipped with an example book.

You may publish any of these.  You cannot publish them under MIT.  They carry CC BY-SA 4.0 and the attribution line above.


The test to apply

Ask one question.  Can a reader rebuild part of the word list from what I am publishing?

If yes, it is CC BY-SA 4.0.

If no, it is yours.


What the corpus does to your numbers

This has nothing to do with licensing.  It is about reading the results.

The list comes from subtitles.  Subtitles are dialogue.  So the ranking measures spoken English, not written English.

You can see it in the ranks.  "you" and "i" both outrank "the".  Clitics get their own entries, 's at rank 6 and 't at rank 10.

Narrative prose measured against this list therefore reads a little high on conversational words and a little low on descriptive ones.

docs/cefr_analysis_notes.txt records what the numbers meant for an actual book.


exclude_proper_nouns.txt
------------------------------------

Proper nouns, invented words and setting-specific coinages.  cefr_bands.py drops these before it bands the text.  A reader does not need to know a character or a place name in order to read past it.

Format.  One word per line.  Blank lines are ignored.  So is anything from a # onwards, whether the # starts the line or follows a word.

The file's own header records how it was built.

The provided file is an example. You are encouraged to delete it and create your own list of proper nouns, invented words, and setting specific terms. That way the reports generated by bookbot.py will be more accurate. You may wish to use an AI agent to scour your draft, here's an example prompt:

    You are helping me build an exclusion list for a vocabulary analysis of
    my manuscript.  The analysis measures how much of my text falls inside
    the common frequency bands of English.  Names and invented words distort
    that measurement, because a reader does not need to know a character or a
    place name in order to read past it, so they are removed before the
    counting starts.

    I will give you the text of my draft.  Read it and return the proper
    nouns, invented words and setting-specific coinages in it.

    Include: character names, including given names, family names and
    nicknames; place names, including planets, stations, cities and regions;
    names of ships, vehicles and vessels; organisations, governments,
    companies and military units; invented species, technologies, materials
    and units of measure; titles and honorifics that exist only in this
    setting; and invented brand names.

    Exclude: ordinary English words, even where they are capitalised because
    they begin a sentence or a line of dialogue; words a reader would meet
    outside my book; and unusual but genuine vocabulary.  Rare and technical
    words are exactly what this analysis exists to measure, so do not drop a
    word merely because it is uncommon.

    Some words are both.  If a word is used in my text as a name and is also
    an ordinary English word, for example a character called Gray or a lizard
    called Gangster, do not put it in the main list.  Put it at the end
    instead, under a separate heading, with the line from my text where it
    appears as a name, so that I can decide.  That decision needs my
    knowledge of the book, so do not make it for me and do not guess.

    Format.  Return one word per line, lowercase, and nothing else.  No
    numbering, no bullets, no headings, no blank lines inside the list, and
    no explanation before or after it.  You may add a comment after a word,
    starting with #, saying what the word is.  List every form that appears
    in my text as its own line, so if both Trelaine and Trelaines occur, give
    both, because the tool matches words exactly and does no stemming.  After
    the main list, leave one blank line, then a line reading
    "# NEEDS A DECISION", then the ambiguous words described above, in the
    same one-per-line format.

A shorter pass is possible once you have a word-count file for your draft: cefr_bands.py --decisions lists the words in it that are rare or absent from the frequency list and that occur at least five times, which is largely names, and that is a much smaller thing to hand an AI than the whole manuscript.


words__agent.txt
------------------------

A word count for Agent, a published novel by the author of this project.  Same {word}{space}{count} format as en_50k.txt.  Produced by bin/vortolisto.py.

This is sample input for cefr_bands.py.  It is not reference data.

Why it is committed.  Agent is published, and the author holds its copyright.  The file lists the words of a book anyone can buy, with the word order gone.  It gives away nothing the book does not.  It also gives cefr_bands.py a real novel to work on, and it lets someone other than the author check the numbers in docs/cefr_analysis_notes.txt.

Licence.  Written for this project, from the author's own text.  Covered by the project LICENSE (MIT).

It is not derived from en_50k.txt.  vortolisto.py counts the words in a manuscript.  It never reads the frequency list.  So the share-alike term does not reach this file.  That is worth saying only because the two files sit in the same directory and look alike.
